# TypeScript Standards

## Module Format

ESM throughout. `"type": "module"` in `package.json`. All imports use `.js` extension (TypeScript resolves to `.ts` at compile time):

```typescript
// ✅
import { parseSpec } from './spec-parser.js';

// ❌
import { parseSpec } from './spec-parser';
```

## Strict Types

- No `any`. Use `unknown` and narrow.
- No default exports. Named exports only.
- Explicit return types on all exported functions.
- `satisfies` over `as` for type assertions.

## LLM Calls

Never call ai-sdk directly from pipelines or adapters. Route through `src/core/llm.ts`:

```typescript
// ✅
import { llmGenerateObject } from '../core/llm.js';
const result = await llmGenerateObject({ schema: MySchema, prompt, system });

// ❌
import { generateObject } from 'ai';
```

## Zod Schemas

All LLM output schemas defined with Zod in the same file that uses them. Schema name matches the type it validates:

```typescript
const specFileSchema = z.object({
  title: z.string(),
  scenarios: z.array(scenarioSchema),
});
type SpecFile = z.infer<typeof specFileSchema>;
```

## Error Handling

Never silently swallow errors. Every `catch` must either re-throw or return a typed error result:

```typescript
// ✅
try {
  return await readFile(path, 'utf-8');
} catch (err) {
  throw new SpecGuardError(`Cannot read spec file: ${path}`, { cause: err });
}

// ❌
try {
  return await readFile(path, 'utf-8');
} catch {}
```

## File I/O

Never call `fs` directly from pipeline or core logic files. Use `src/core/writer.ts` for writes, `src/core/reader.ts` for reads. This makes pipelines testable without touching the filesystem.

## Exit Codes

CLI commands exit with typed codes (see `src/core/exit-codes.ts`). Never `process.exit(1)` — always use the named constant.
